{"subject":"Anatomy","sourceTitle":"All In One Online","title":"PrepLadder · 52.Previous Year Questions","deckName":"created by tele @Neet_pg_bot::prep::Qbank::Anatomy::52.Previous_Year_Questions","questions":[{"id":"all-in-one-1760436512004","topic":"PrepLadder · 52.Previous Year Questions","number":1,"stemHtml":"The marked structure serves as a surface landmark for which anatomical structure?\n{{caption_text}}<br/><img src=\"images/image_98_44_1.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pineal gland"},{"label":"B","text":"Wernicke's area"},{"label":"C","text":"Stem of lateral sulcus"},{"label":"D","text":"Internal carotid artery siphon"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Stem of lateral sulcus","solutionHtml":"0e21321692777191739bd99191\"&amp;gt;lateral <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2a56f52a921692777199df43853eba\">sulcus</span> (Sylvian fissure) serves as a key surface landmark in the brain.\n<li>It separates the <strong>frontal and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"637ea4d17516927772003be2b2aae4\">temporal</span> lobes</strong> and extends posteriorly towards the <strong>insular cortex</strong>.</li>\n<li>It is an important landmark for neurosurgical approaches and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d100eb4ed81692777193b7f47b5cba\">neurovascular</span> structures.</li>\n<li>The <strong>middle cerebral <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a66cd3adaa1692777182e77cb23021\">artery</span> (MCA)</strong> runs within the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc380e21321692777191739bd99191\">lateral</span> sulcus, supplying a major portion of the cerebral cortex.</li>\n\n<p><br/>\n<br/>\n </p>\n<p><strong>Lateral <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2a56f52a921692777199df43853eba\">Sulcus</span> (Sylvian Fissure) and Its <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"116f86834a1692777181a914379270\">Anatomical</span> Importance</strong></p>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Feature</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Description</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Location</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Separates the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"35aa36279d16927772031c7ce8d6e5\">frontal</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"637ea4d17516927772003be2b2aae4\">temporal</span> lobes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Extends to</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Insular <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cab8787ce916927771852a7e72f34d\">cortex</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"51df38de3d1692777194ced4c4bab5\">parietal</span> lobe</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Contains</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Middle cerebral <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a66cd3adaa1692777182e77cb23021\">artery</span> (MCA)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Functions</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Landmark for neurosurgery, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"721937292f1692777185048f04ce29\">cortical</span> mapping</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Related Clinical Importance</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>MCA strokes affect motor, sensory, and language functions</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div style=\"text-align:center\">\n<figure class=\"caption_element\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"438\" src=\"images/exp_98_44_1.jpg\" width=\"907\"/>\n<figcaption>{{caption_text}}</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n<div style=\"text-align:center\">\n<figure class=\"caption_element\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"454\" src=\"images/exp_98_44_2.jpg\" width=\"457\"/>\n<figcaption>{{caption_text}}</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Pineal <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4744bb90c21692777188c8957f9faa\">gland</span> (Option A):</strong> Incorrect because the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8d0ddb3b3c1692777195823fa13daa\">pineal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4744bb90c21692777188c8957f9faa\">gland</span> is located near the <strong>posterior midline of the brain</strong>, not associated with the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc380e21321692777191739bd99191\">lateral</span> sulcus.</p>\n<p><strong>Wernicke’s area (Option B):</strong> Incorrect because Wernicke’s area is found in the <strong>posterior superior <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"637ea4d17516927772003be2b2aae4\">temporal</span> gyrus</strong>, although related to the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc380e21321692777191739bd99191\">lateral</span> sulcus, it is not its direct landmark.</p>\n<p><strong>Internal <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8593e48e0416927771834eaedb0bfd\">carotid</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a66cd3adaa1692777182e77cb23021\">artery</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9d59eae3ce16927771982e99e0035f\">siphon</span> (Option D):</strong> Incorrect because the ICA <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9d59eae3ce16927771982e99e0035f\">siphon</span> is an <strong>S-shaped bend in the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"34f284128d1692777183e1cf6e17d7\">cavernous</span> segment of the internal <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8593e48e0416927771834eaedb0bfd\">carotid</span> artery</strong>, located near the base of the skull, not directly related to the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc380e21321692777191739bd99191\">lateral</span> sulcus.</p><br/><img src=\"images/exp_98_44_1.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_98_44_2.jpg\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Gray’s Anatomy, 42nd Edition, Pages 395, 396<br/>Vishram Singh, Anatomy of Head, Neck, Brain, Volume 3, Pages 389-391</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436512006","topic":"PrepLadder · 52.Previous Year Questions","number":2,"stemHtml":"The bone shown in the image is not associated with which of the following types of joints?<br/><img src=\"images/image_105_3_1.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syndesmosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Symphysis"},{"label":"C","text":"Plane synovial joint"},{"label":"D","text":"Synchondrosis"}],"answer":"D","answerText":"Synchondrosis","solutionHtml":"<p><strong>Correct Option D - Synchondrosis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The image shown above is of vertebra</li>\n<li>Synchondrosis is a type of joint where the bones are connected by <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2ce88e8a0169277718988f5bc31b8\">hyaline</span> cartilage. These joints are usually temporary and found in growing bones, such as the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"dc486020a916927771874098596bbc\">epiphyseal</span> plates (growth plates) in children and the first <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4deaa4422f16927771993d3c380a8c\">sternocostal</span> joint that unites the first rib to the sternum</li>\n<li>Synchondrosis or primary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2a990a23c1692777183c6b47c7819\">cartilaginous</span> joints are not present in the given image</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A - Syndesmosis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3e76634cff169277719913c5f4bf9e\">syndesmosis</span> is a type of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5d6dfa73b316927772028f3cd60f7f\">fibrous</span> joint where bones are connected by a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"301e3b01f01692777191aced6dc342\">ligament</span> or a sheet of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"67e237a57c16927771859803f93ab3\">connective</span> tissue.</li>\n<li>The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8202a376b11692777199029f89afab\">supraspinous</span> ligament, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ecfea7a9d416927771908bfc754ecd\">interspinous</span> ligament, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c6d5162a2816927771918bfaa5d7ec\">ligamentum</span> flavum (Connecting the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9864ad42b21692777191febeee1002\">lamina</span> of adjacent bones). <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a5c6cb079d16927771967e62ffee72\">Posterior</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7ba18aa5e616927771918a45051a40\">longitudinal</span> ligament, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1ba6a9cfe11692777181950edd7a6d\">anterior</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7ba18aa5e616927771918a45051a40\">longitudinal</span> ligament, these ligaments connects the adjacent <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9c0b99fa0816927772010e947b4ef6\">vertebrae</span> forming syndesmosis</li>\n</ul>\n<h4><strong>Option B - Symphysis:</strong></h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition</strong>: A <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"48b6a0a5fb1692777199da12372b14\">symphysis</span> is a type of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2a990a23c1692777183c6b47c7819\">cartilaginous</span> joint where the bones are connected by fibrocartilage.</li>\n<li>Examples:\n\t<ul>\n<li>The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fff48ca9da16927771966fd16bbacc\">pubic</span> symphysis.</li>\n<li>The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"95a8e326831692777190c5101eb835\">intervertebral</span> discs.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4><strong>Option C - Plane <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d27f9c7a1b16927771999b1679d949\">Synovial</span> Joint:</strong></h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A plane <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d27f9c7a1b16927771999b1679d949\">synovial</span> joint, also known as a gliding joint, involves flat or nearly flat <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cd001152fe16927771823275439a2d\">articular</span> surfaces that allow bones to slide over one another.</li>\n<li>The joint between the superior <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cd001152fe16927771823275439a2d\">articular</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"25d29169e3169277720246b37b93ef\">facet</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"49c4ef5b9d1692777190c0277f8344\">inferior</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cd001152fe16927771823275439a2d\">articular</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"25d29169e3169277720246b37b93ef\">facet</span> is plain <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d27f9c7a1b16927771999b1679d949\">synovial</span> joint and thus makes it an incorrect choice</li>\n</ul>\n<br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436512008","topic":"PrepLadder · 52.Previous Year Questions","number":3,"stem":"Which of the following is the type of joint between epiphysis and diaphysis of a long bone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Symphysis"},{"label":"B","text":"Synchondrosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Synostosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Syndesmosis"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"Synchondrosis","solutionHtml":"y <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2a990a23c1692777183c6b47c7819\">cartilaginous</span> joint where the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0fa073bd8016927771872ee5b57674\">epiphysis</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"604558eae21692777186613f3915a9\">diaphysis</span> of a long bone are connected by a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3480d51034169277718862050fed12\">growth plate</span> made of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2ce88e8a0169277718988f5bc31b8\">hyaline</span> cartilage.\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:624px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<h4><strong>Primary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2a990a23c1692777183c6b47c7819\">Cartilaginous</span> Joints (Synchondrosis)</strong></h4>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<h4><strong>Secondary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2a990a23c1692777183c6b47c7819\">Cartilaginous</span> Joints (Symphysis) (Option A)</strong></h4>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Immobile</li>\n<li>Occur where <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3be8c9fc131692777194e15d6aeef3\">ossification</span> centres remain separated by <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2ce88e8a0169277718988f5bc31b8\">hyaline</span> cartilage.</li>\n<li>Found in bones that <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a1b06f401f1692777203a53393ba98\">form</span> from more than one <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3be8c9fc131692777194e15d6aeef3\">ossification</span> centre.</li>\n<li>Hyaline <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c6f61cdbe41692777183fdc8f95f96\">cartilage</span> here can ossify with age, leading to <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a7dfeb385e1692777203302bcf142d\">fusion</span> when growth is completed.</li>\n<li>Temporary and mainly for growth.</li>\n<li>Mostly associated with growth plates. The joint between the first rib and the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7f52cf1a7f1692777191d49738d6b9\">manubrium</span> is a permanent synchondrosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"4167\" src=\"images/exp_106_144_1.jpg\" width=\"4167\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Slightly mobile</li>\n<li>Defined by a pad or disc of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"50747b27421692777202260a27b878\">fibrocartilage</span> between articulating bones, e.g., <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"95a8e326831692777190c5101eb835\">intervertebral</span> discs.</li>\n<li>Built to handle a variety of stresses (compression, tension, etc.).</li>\n<li>Permanent and provide limited movement.</li>\n<li>Found along the midline (e.g., <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fff48ca9da16927771966fd16bbacc\">pubic</span> symphysis, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"95a8e326831692777190c5101eb835\">intervertebral</span> joints). Strong ligaments reinforce the joints and are key to stability and movement.</li>\n<li>Symphyses are built for durability and movement, but they also allow growth in areas like the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"95a8e326831692777190c5101eb835\">intervertebral</span> discs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"4167\" src=\"images/exp_106_144_2.jpg\" width=\"4167\"/></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Synostosis (Option C): </strong>Synostosis refers to the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a7dfeb385e1692777203302bcf142d\">fusion</span> of bones where the original <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c6f61cdbe41692777183fdc8f95f96\">cartilage</span> is replaced entirely by bone.</p>\n<p><strong>Syndesmosis (Option D): </strong>A <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3e76634cff169277719913c5f4bf9e\">syndesmosis</span> is a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5d6dfa73b316927772028f3cd60f7f\">fibrous</span> joint where bones are connected by a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"301e3b01f01692777191aced6dc342\">ligament</span> or an <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"72b8cffc701692777190c03a6968ab\">interosseous</span> membrane, allowing for limited movement.</p><br/><img src=\"images/exp_106_144_1.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_106_144_2.jpg\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Gray&rsquo;s Anatomy, 42nd Edition, Pg.102,106\nBD Chaurasia 8th edition, volume 1, pg 155</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436512010","topic":"PrepLadder · 52.Previous Year Questions","number":4,"stemHtml":"Which part of this bone is palpable in the infraclavicular fossa?<br/><img src=\"images/anatomy-l021-q055-p0836-stem-1.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"1","solutionHtml":"<p>The <strong>coracoid process (1)</strong> is the <strong>only <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"bd478e4ff816927771949528846af9\">palpable</span> part of the scapula</strong> in the <strong>infraclavicular fossa</strong>. It lies deep to the <strong>clavicle</strong>, but its tip can be felt by pressing below the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc380e21321692777191739bd99191\">lateral</span> third of the clavicle.</p>\n<p><strong>Palpable Bony Landmarks of the Scapula</strong></p>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:574px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Structure</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Palpability</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Spine of scapula</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Easily <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"bd478e4ff816927771949528846af9\">palpable</span> posteriorly.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Acromion</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Most <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc380e21321692777191739bd99191\">lateral</span> and superior part, prominent.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Coracoid process</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Palpable in the <strong>infraclavicular fossa</strong>, serves as an <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ccd357daaf16927771825777ea6a0d\">attachment</span> site.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Inferior angle</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Palpable at the lower <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"db46007ad21692777192dcc160a2e2\">medial</span> end of the scapula.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Acromion (Option B-2): Incorrect</strong>, as it is <strong>palpable over the shoulder</strong>, not in the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"71409dc88a1692777190aedbc558b6\">infraclavicular</span> fossa.</p>\n<p><strong>Lateral angle of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"153f2ea4be16927771981ce5d9171f\">scapula</span> (Option C-3): Incorrect</strong>, as it is <strong>posteriorly palpable</strong>, not infraclavicular.</p>\n<p><strong>Inferior angle (Option D-4): Incorrect</strong>, as it is <strong>only <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"bd478e4ff816927771949528846af9\">palpable</span> at the lower scapular border</strong>.</p>\n<br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Gray's Anatomy: The Anatomical Basis of Clinical Practice, 41st Edition - Page nos: 787, 803<br/>Gray's Anatomy: The Anatomical Basis of Clinical Practice, 42nd Edition - Page no: 884, 896</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436512012","topic":"PrepLadder · 52.Previous Year Questions","number":5,"stemHtml":"What type of joint is seen in the labeled structure?<br/><img src=\"images/anatomy-l041-q009-p1616-stem-1.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syndesmosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Primary cartilaginous joints"},{"label":"C","text":"Fibrous joint"},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary cartilaginous joint"}],"answer":"D","answerText":"Secondary cartilaginous joint","solutionHtml":"fd16bbacc\"&amp;gt;pubic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"48b6a0a5fb1692777199da12372b14\">symphysis</span> is a joint sandwiched between your left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7cd6eb19341692777195d83aafdcb3\">pelvic</span> bone and right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7cd6eb19341692777195d83aafdcb3\">pelvic</span> bone. It is a secondary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2a990a23c1692777183c6b47c7819\">cartilaginous</span> joint. The secondary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2a990a23c1692777183c6b47c7819\">cartilaginous</span> joints are permanent joints, which are slightly movable and their movements depend upon the sufficient amount of white-fibro <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c6f61cdbe41692777183fdc8f95f96\">cartilage</span> tissues.\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Primary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2a990a23c1692777183c6b47c7819\">Cartilaginous</span> Joint (Option B)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Secondary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2a990a23c1692777183c6b47c7819\">Cartilaginous</span> Joint</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Sutures</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Syndesmoses</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>(Option A)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Gomphosis</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none\">\n<ul>\n<li>Joint between <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0fa073bd8016927771872ee5b57674\">epiphysis</span> and diaphysis</li>\n<li>Between basiocciput and basisphenoid</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none\">\n<ul>\n<li>Symphysis menti</li>\n<li>Symphysis pubis</li>\n<li>Manubrio sternal</li>\n<li>Joint between vertebral bodies, xiphisternal joint, midline <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"95a8e326831692777190c5101eb835\">intervertebral</span> joint</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Serrate</strong>: Sagittal</li>\n<li><strong>Denticulate</strong>: Lambdoid</li>\n<li><strong>Squamous</strong>:Temporoparietal</li>\n<li><strong>Plane</strong>: Between <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1b6ebc94b416927771947d3c404756\">palatine</span> process of maxillae</li>\n<li><strong>Schindylesis</strong>: Between <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"bd22f326b11692777197600574cc70\">rostrum</span> of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"32aabfe5711692777199d68bd57d67\">sphenoid</span> and upper margin of vomer</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none\">\n<p>Inferior <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fecef2f7c41692777200740aa13322\">tibiofibular</span> joint</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none\">\n<p>Tooth fit in the socket</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Fibrous joints are classified into: (Option C)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sutures,</li>\n<li>Syndesmoses</li>\n<li>Gomphosis</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n</ul><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Gray’s Anatomy, 42nd Edition, Pg.101,102<br/>Snells Anatomy, 10th edition, Pg 13</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436512014","topic":"PrepLadder · 52.Previous Year Questions","number":6,"stemHtml":"In the labeled structure, what kind of joint is present?<br/><img src=\"images/image_122_215_1.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sutures"},{"label":"B","text":"Primary cartilaginous joints"},{"label":"C","text":"Fibrous joint"},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary cartilaginous joint"}],"answer":"D","answerText":"Secondary cartilaginous joint","solutionHtml":"fd16bbacc\"&amp;gt;pubic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"48b6a0a5fb1692777199da12372b14\">symphysis</span> is a joint sandwiched between your left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7cd6eb19341692777195d83aafdcb3\">pelvic</span> bone and right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7cd6eb19341692777195d83aafdcb3\">pelvic</span> bone. It is a secondary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2a990a23c1692777183c6b47c7819\">cartilaginous</span> joint. The secondary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2a990a23c1692777183c6b47c7819\">cartilaginous</span> joints are permanent joints, which are slightly movable and their movements depend upon the sufficient amount of white-fibro <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c6f61cdbe41692777183fdc8f95f96\">cartilage</span> tissues.\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Primary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2a990a23c1692777183c6b47c7819\">Cartilaginous</span> Joint (Option B)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Secondary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a2a990a23c1692777183c6b47c7819\">Cartilaginous</span> Joint</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Sutures (Option A)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Syndesmoses</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Gomphosis</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none\">\n<ul>\n<li>Joint between <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0fa073bd8016927771872ee5b57674\">epiphysis</span> and diaphysis</li>\n<li>Between basiocciput and basisphenoid</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none\">\n<ul>\n<li>Symphysis menti</li>\n<li>Symphysis pubis</li>\n<li>Manubrio sternal</li>\n<li>Joint between vertebral bodies, xiphisternal joint, midline <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"95a8e326831692777190c5101eb835\">intervertebral</span> joint</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Serrate:</strong> Sagittal</li>\n<li><strong>Denticulate:</strong> Lambdoid</li>\n<li><strong>Squamous:</strong>Temporoparietal</li>\n<li><strong>Plane:</strong> Between <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1b6ebc94b416927771947d3c404756\">palatine</span> process of maxillae</li>\n<li><strong>Schindylesis:</strong> Between <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"bd22f326b11692777197600574cc70\">rostrum</span> of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"32aabfe5711692777199d68bd57d67\">sphenoid</span> and upper margin of vomer</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none\">\n<ul>\n<li>Inferior <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fecef2f7c41692777200740aa13322\">tibiofibular</span> joint</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none\">\n<ul>\n<li>Tooth fit in the socket</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Fibrous joints are classified into: (Option C) </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sutures,</li>\n<li>Syndesmoses</li>\n<li>Gomphosis</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n</ul>\n<p> </p><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Gray’s Anatomy, 42nd Edition, Pg.101,102<br/>Snells Anatomy, 10th edition, Pg 13</div>","tags":""}]}